      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manley_Hot_Springs%2C_Alaska",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The record low temperature of −73 °F was recorded in January 1989, while the record high of 93 °F was in June 1969.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manley_Hot_Springs%2C_Alaska",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town's name was changed to Manley Hot Springs in 1957.",
